'''tony-hoop''' (tō′ni-höp), ''n.'' Same as ''tonnihood.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''too'''<sup>1</sup> (tö), ''adv.'' [Early mod. E. also ''to;'' < ME. ''to,'' < AS. ''tō,'' too, = G. ''zu,'' etc., too, more than enough; < AS. ''tō,'' prep.: see ''to''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. Over; more than enough: noting excess, and qualifying an adjective or an adverb.

{{smaller block|Farewell, Alinda:<br>I am ''too'' full to speak more, and ''too'' wretched.{{right|''Fletcher'', Loyal Subject, iv. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|He names this word Colledge ''too'' often, and his discourse bears ''too'' much on the Vniuersity.{{right|''Bp. Earle'', Micro-cosmographie, A Downe-right Scholler.}}}}

{{smaller block|[''Too'' in this sense is sometimes erroneously used to qualify a verb.<br>I'll look within no more:<br>I have ''too'' trusted to my own wild wants,<br>''Too'' trusted to myself, to intuition.{{right|''Browning'', Pauline.]}}}}

2. Exceedingly; extremely: an intensive use.

{{smaller block|They continually pretend to have some sovereign power over that empire, and yet are ''too'' happy to be at peace with it.{{right|''Brougham.''}}}}

3. In addition; also; furthermore; moreover.

{{smaller block|Pretty and witty, wild, and yet, ''too'', gentle.{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., iii. 1. 110.}}}}

{{smaller block|What, will these young gentlemen ''too'' help us to catch this fresh salmon, ha?{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, iv. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|Never was there a more complete victory, achieved ''too'' within the space of little more than an hour.{{right|''Prescott'', Ferd. and Isa., ii. 12.}}}}

